const chk=(q,options,why)=>({t:'check',q,options,why});

export const S1={title:'How Clinical Specialty works and is scored',hook:'Clinical Specialty is a career-awareness event with three products built around one health career: a pre-judged portfolio, a video of a clinical skill and a five-minute live presentation. Every point on the 155-point rubric is earned before you walk into the room or in those five minutes, so knowing the rubric is the first skill.',objectives:['Describe the three products of Clinical Specialty and how they connect to one career.','Explain Round One and Round Two, the upload deadline and what is new for 2026–27.','Break down the 155 points by rating-sheet item.','Build a realistic preparation timeline.'],sections:[
['One career, three products',[
'Clinical Specialty asks a solo competitor to choose **one health career** and use it for everything: a **career portfolio** (Round One, pre-judged), a **skill video** of a clinical skill performed in that career, and a **five-minute presentation** to judges (Round Two) during which all or part of the video is shown. The event exists to build **career awareness**—you are judged on how well you understand the career, its training, its place in the healthcare system and one representative skill. It is open to Secondary and Postsecondary/Collegiate divisions.',
'Two changes are new for 2026–27: an **in-person Work-based Learning Form** has been added alongside the virtual versions, and the presentation is now **five minutes**. Competitors must wear business attire or the HOSA uniform (bonus points for proper dress), bring photo ID, a **battery-powered electronic device** that plays the skill video and, optionally, notecards. Electronic notecards must be on a **different device** from the one showing the video, and only the skill video may be shown to judges.']],
['Round One: the pre-judged portfolio',[
'The portfolio is uploaded as **one combined PDF** to the HOSA Digital Upload System by **May 15 at midnight EST** for the International Leadership Conference, with no exceptions. A file that is not a single PDF is not viewed and earns no points, and links judges cannot open earn nothing. If your state used the upload system, you must upload **again** for ILC; if it did not, you are still responsible for the ILC upload. Competitors who do not upload are **not eligible for Round Two** and receive no appointment time. State Leadership Conference rules vary, so competitors check with their local advisor.',
{t:'table',caption:'Round One portfolio rating sheet (85 points)',head:['Item','Points'],rows:[['1. Title page','5'],['2. Work-based Learning Form','5'],['3. Career summary content','10'],['4. Education, training, association and career data','10'],['5. Interview summary','10'],['6. Learning outcomes summary','10'],['7. Skill checklist','10'],['8. Reference page','5'],['9. Neatness (formatting, grammar, spelling)','5'],['10. Formatting (header, font, spacing, margins)','5'],['11. Maximum pages (no more than 12 total)','5'],['12. Skill not duplicated','5']]}]],
['Round Two: the presentation',[
'All competitors who uploaded advance to Round Two. You have **five minutes total**, including any video, and the timekeeper announces when **one minute** remains and stops you at five. The presentation should explain **the career** (job responsibilities, training and employment opportunities), **how you selected it**, **how your strengths and preferences relate to it** and **how the career fits into the healthcare system**, and it should show the **skill video** while you discuss the performance. You may fast-forward, reverse or use prepared clips. HOSA provides no devices, Wi-Fi or electricity, and the screen must be visible to judges up to **five feet** away.',
{t:'table',caption:'Round Two presentation rating sheet (70 points)',head:['Area','Item','Points'],rows:[['A. Content','Understanding of the career','10'],['A. Content','Explanation of how the career was selected','10'],['A. Content','Relating personal strengths and preferences','10'],['A. Content','How the career fits into the healthcare system','10'],['B. Delivery','Voice','5'],['B. Delivery','Stage presence','5'],['B. Delivery','Diction, pronunciation and grammar','5'],['D. Skill video','Video inclusion (audio, visuals, competitor performing, used to show understanding)','15']]},
'Round One and Round Two scores are added for a **total of 155 points**. Ties are broken by the rating-sheet items with the highest point values, in descending order—so the 15-point video item and the 10-point content items matter most.']],
['Where the points are',[
'Notice that **40 of the 85 portfolio points** come from five sections worth 5 points each that are almost entirely about **following directions**: a complete title page, a complete Work-based Learning Form, a reference page, correct formatting and staying within the page limit, plus a skill that is not duplicated. These are all-or-nothing items—there is no partial credit for a header that is missing page numbers or a skill that appears on the forbidden list. The remaining points reward depth: complete, clear and comprehensive content that goes beyond what anyone can find online.',
sort('Compliance item or depth item?',['All-or-nothing compliance','Scored for depth and quality'],[['Running header and page numbers',0],['Portfolio within 12 pages',0],['Skill not on another event’s list',0],['Interview reflects information only a professional could share',1],['Career summary covers duties and employment characteristics',1],['Learning outcomes show insight into the work environment',1],['Title page lists every required item',0],['Skill checklist broken into logical sub-parts',1]]),
chk('A portfolio is uploaded as three separate PDFs. What happens?',['Judges will not view it, and it earns no points','Judges score only the first file','Points are deducted for neatness only','It is accepted if uploaded before May 15'],'The upload must be one combined PDF.')]],
['Planning backward from May 15',[
'Strong competitors work backward from the deadline. The interview and **at least 8 hours of job shadowing** depend on other people’s schedules, so they are arranged first. The skill must be learned from a **verifiable text resource** and practiced until it is smooth before it is recorded. Writing, formatting and proofreading take longer than expected, and the presentation needs rehearsal with a timer.',
order('A sensible preparation sequence',['Choose one health career and confirm it meets the rules','Select a skill that is not duplicated and find a verifiable text resource','Research the career with authoritative sources','Arrange and complete the interview and 8+ hours of job shadowing','Write the skill checklist and practice the skill','Record and edit the skill video','Write, format and proofread the portfolio; combine into one PDF','Upload before May 15 and rehearse the five-minute presentation']),
kase('A deadline decision',{a:{text:'It is May 10. Your portfolio is written, but your shadowing host has not returned the signed Work-based Learning Form. What do you do?',choices:[['Contact the host politely today, explain the deadline and ask for the signed form','b','Correct. The form must be completed and signed by the host.',true],['Fill in and sign the form yourself to save time','b','The form must be completed by the professional shadowed; falsifying it is dishonest and disqualifying.',false]]},b:{text:'The host returns the form on May 13. What remains before the upload?',choices:[['Scan it, insert it in the portfolio, combine everything into one PDF and check every page','c','Correct. Verify the single PDF opens and every page is legible.',true],['Upload the form as a separate file','c','Separate files are not viewed.',false]]},c:{text:'You upload one combined PDF on May 14, confirm it opens, and save a copy for your presentation practice.',end:true}})]],
["Integrity, general rules and competition day",["Clinical Specialty is built on **honest documentation**. The interview must have happened with the person named; the Work-based Learning Form must be completed and signed by the host; the hours must be real; and the skill must be performed by the competitor in the video. HOSA’s General Rules and Regulations apply to every event, and dishonesty—fabricated forms, plagiarized text, someone else performing the skill—can lead to disqualification. Original writing matters too: research must be paraphrased in your own words and cited, and quotations must be marked and attributed.","On competition day, report to the event site at your **appointment time** with **photo ID**, dressed in business attire or the official HOSA uniform for the dress bonus. Bring your charged device with the video cued and optional notecards. The judges have already scored your portfolio; Round Two is your chance to bring it to life. Arrive early enough to compose yourself, greet the judges confidently, stand where they can see both you and the screen, and thank them when you finish. After the conference, HOSA scholarships (applications open January 1) and free lifetime alumni membership keep the career journey going.",{"t":"callout","kind":"exam","title":"A rubric habit","text":"Before you finish any portfolio section, open the rating sheet and check every criterion in the “Excellent” column. If a criterion is not visible on the page, a judge cannot award it."},{"t":"check","q":"Which action violates event integrity?","options":["Signing the Work-based Learning Form for the host","Paraphrasing and citing BLS data","Using a model to simulate the skill","Showing only part of the skill video"],"why":"The form must be completed by the professional shadowed."}]]
],takeaways:['One career drives the portfolio, skill video and five-minute presentation; the event builds career awareness.','Round One: one PDF uploaded by May 15 (85 points); Round Two: five minutes including video (70 points); 155 total.','Compliance items are all-or-nothing; plan the interview and 8 hours of shadowing early.'],terms:[['Pre-judged','Scored by judges before the conference from the uploaded file.'],['Work-based learning','Learning in a real workplace, such as job shadowing.'],['Tiebreaker','Rating-sheet items compared from highest point value downward.']],mcqs:[q('How long is the Round Two presentation in 2026–27?','Five minutes including the skill video','Ten minutes plus questions','Seven minutes without the video','Fifteen minutes','The time changed to five minutes this year.'),q('By when must the ILC portfolio be uploaded?','May 15 at midnight EST','June 1','The day of competition','April 1','There are no exceptions.'),q('How many total points are possible?','155','100','85','200','85 for the portfolio plus 70 for the presentation.'),q('What happens to a competitor who does not upload a portfolio?','They are not eligible for Round Two','They present for half points','They can bring a printed copy','They lose only 5 points','No upload means no appointment time.'),q('Electronic notecards must be:','On a different device from the one showing the video','Shown to the judges','On the same tablet as the video','Printed on the title page','Only the skill video may be shown to judges.'),q('Which item is worth the most in Round Two?','Skill video inclusion (15 points)','Voice (5 points)','Stage presence (5 points)','Diction (5 points)','The video item is worth 15.'),q('How are ties broken?','By the highest-point rating-sheet items in descending order','By coin toss','By the youngest competitor','By total word count','This makes high-value items decisive.'),q('What did HOSA add for 2026–27?','An in-person Work-based Learning Form','A written test','A team option','A required poster','Virtual versions remain available.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the structure of the Clinical Specialty event, including both rounds, the upload requirements, and how points are distributed. Then describe a preparation plan that protects the most points.',rubric:[['Three products and one career',['portfolio','video','presentation','one career','career']],['Round details and deadline',['pdf','may 15','pre-judged','five minutes','5 minutes']],['Point distribution',['155','85','70','15']],['Preparation plan',['interview','shadow','8 hours','practice','proofread','rehearse']]],model:'Clinical Specialty is built around one health career used for three products: a portfolio, a skill video and a live presentation. In Round One, the portfolio is uploaded as a single combined PDF by May 15 at midnight EST and pre-judged for 85 points; anyone who does not upload cannot compete in Round Two. In Round Two, the competitor has five minutes, including the skill video shown on their own battery-powered device, to explain the career, how it was chosen, how their strengths fit it and how it fits into the healthcare system, for 70 points, making 155 in total. Many portfolio items—title page, Work-based Learning Form, reference page, formatting, page limit and a non-duplicated skill—are all-or-nothing, so I would check them against the rating sheet. My plan would be to choose the career and skill first, schedule the interview and at least 8 hours of shadowing early, learn the skill from a verifiable text, record the video, write and proofread the portfolio, upload early and rehearse with a timer.'},sources:[RULES,UPLOAD,EHC]};

export const S2={title:'Choosing a health career',hook:'“Forensics” is not a career, but “medical examiner” is. “Firefighter” is a public-safety career, not a health career. The first decision in Clinical Specialty—which career—determines whether every later product is eligible, so it needs to be made with the rules and a map of the health professions in hand.',objectives:['Apply HOSA’s four career-selection rules.','Distinguish a specific health career from an area of specialty or a non-health career.','Use the health science pathways to explore options.','Match a career to personal strengths, interests and values.'],sections:[
['The career-selection rules',[
'HOSA’s guidelines set four requirements. First, choose **one** health career and use it for the portfolio, video and presentation. Second, the career must be a **health** career—firefighters, flight attendants and special education teachers, for example, are not. Third, choose a **specific health career, not an area of specialty**: “medical examiner” is a career, “forensics” is not; “respiratory therapist” is a career, “pulmonology” is a field. Fourth, the career must have **at least one clinical skill** that the competitor can learn and demonstrate. HOSA points to the **National Consortium for Health Science Education (NCHSE)** and **Explore Health Careers** for sample lists and notes that the NCHSE list has been updated to match **Advance CTE career clusters**, so competitors should check carefully that their choice meets the criteria.',
sort('Eligible career or not?',['Eligible specific health career','Not eligible as written'],[['Respiratory therapist',0],['Medical examiner',0],['Forensics',1],['Firefighter',1],['Occupational therapy assistant',0],['Cardiology',1],['Audiologist',0],['Flight attendant',1]])]],
['Mapping the health professions',[
'Health careers are often grouped into pathways. **Therapeutic services** change a patient’s health over time—nurses, physicians, dental hygienists, physical therapists, respiratory therapists, pharmacists and athletic trainers. **Diagnostic services** create a picture of health status—radiologic technologists, sonographers, medical laboratory scientists, audiologists and phlebotomists. **Health informatics** manages information—health information technicians, medical coders and health data analysts. **Support services** create a safe, therapeutic environment—biomedical equipment technicians, central service technicians and environmental services. **Biotechnology research and development** studies disease and develops treatments—research scientists, genetic counselors and clinical trial coordinators. For Clinical Specialty, careers with hands-on clinical skills are usually easiest to demonstrate, but any eligible career with a learnable clinical skill qualifies.',
match('Match the career to its pathway',[['Diagnostic medical sonographer','Diagnostic services'],['Physical therapist assistant','Therapeutic services'],['Medical coder','Health informatics'],['Central service technician','Support services'],['Genetic counselor','Biotechnology research and development'],['Dental hygienist','Therapeutic services']])]],
['Matching the career to you',[
'Judges award up to 10 points for relating **personal strengths and preferences** to the career, with full credit for **four or more strong connections** between your attributes and the job’s demands. Start by listing your strengths (for example, steady hands, calm under pressure, enjoying science, liking to teach, attention to detail), your preferences (patient contact or behind the scenes, hospital or community, predictable hours or shifts, physical activity) and your values (helping children, solving puzzles, independence). Then compare them with the career’s **tasks, work context and skills** in **O*NET OnLine**, which lists each occupation’s duties, required abilities and work styles.',
'Strong connections are specific: “I volunteer at a summer camp for children with asthma, and pediatric respiratory therapists spend much of their day teaching families to use inhalers” is far stronger than “I like helping people.” The same connections become the backbone of your presentation’s explanation of why you chose the career.',
flip('Turn a vague reason into a specific connection',[['“I like science.”','“I enjoyed calculating dilutions in chemistry, and medical laboratory scientists prepare reagents and controls daily.”'],['“I want to help people.”','“I tutor younger students, and occupational therapists coach patients through tasks step by step.”'],['“I’m good with my hands.”','“I build models and repair bikes, and dental lab technicians fabricate crowns with fine motor precision.”'],['“I like fast-paced work.”','“I play point guard and make quick decisions, which matches the pace of an emergency department.”']])]],
['Checking eligibility before you commit',[
'Before you commit, test the career against the full plan. Can you identify a **clinical skill** performed by this professional that is **not** on HOSA’s list of skills used in other events? Can you learn it from a **verifiable text** and perform or simulate it safely on yourself, a volunteer or a model? Can you find a practicing professional to **interview** (not your instructor or HOSA advisor) and a site for **8 hours of shadowing**? Can you locate reliable data on education, credentials, employment and outlook? If any answer is no, choose a different career now rather than in April.',
kase('Specialty or career?',{a:{text:'A student wants to compete in Clinical Specialty with “sports medicine.” What should their advisor say?',choices:[['Sports medicine is a field; choose a specific career such as athletic trainer','b','Correct. HOSA requires a specific career, not a specialty area.',true],['Sports medicine is fine as written','b','It names an area of practice, not a career.',false]]},b:{text:'The student chooses athletic trainer. They plan to demonstrate ankle taping for inversion. Is this allowed?',choices:[['No—ankle inversion taping is a Sports Medicine event skill, so choose another athletic-training skill','c','Correct. Skills in other HOSA events may not be duplicated.',true],['Yes, any skill from the career is allowed','c','Duplicated skills earn zero for item 12 and undermine the portfolio.',false]]},c:{text:'The student finds a different athletic-training skill in a published textbook, confirms it is not on the list and secures a shadowing site at a high school athletic training room.',end:true}})]],
['Researching broadly before narrowing',[
'Exploration resources include the **Explore Health Careers** profiles (education, salary and outlook for dozens of careers), the **Bureau of Labor Statistics Occupational Outlook Handbook** healthcare section, O*NET OnLine and professional association websites, which often feature “a day in the life” articles and student resources. Talking to school counselors, attending HOSA career sessions and visiting local healthcare facilities can surface careers students have never heard of, such as perfusionist, orthotist and prosthetist, or nuclear medicine technologist. A career that is unfamiliar to judges can be memorable—as long as you can explain it clearly and fit it into the healthcare system.',
chk('Which choice names a specific health career with a demonstrable clinical skill?',['Certified orthotist','Forensics','Public safety','Oncology'],'Orthotists are a specific profession; the others are fields or non-health areas.')]],
["Careers worth discovering",["Many strong Clinical Specialty portfolios feature careers judges rarely see, because they reveal how wide the health system is. A **perfusionist** operates the heart–lung machine during cardiac surgery. An **orthotist and prosthetist** designs and fits braces and artificial limbs. A **nuclear medicine technologist** prepares radioactive tracers and performs imaging scans. A **child life specialist** helps children and families cope with hospitalization through play and preparation. A **genetic counselor** helps families understand inherited conditions and testing. A **polysomnographic technologist** monitors patients overnight in sleep labs. An **anesthesiologist assistant** works within the anesthesia care team. An **orthoptist** evaluates and treats eye-movement disorders. Each has distinct training, credentials and associations—and at least one hands-on skill to explore.","Unusual is not automatically better: you still need a learnable, non-duplicated clinical skill, access to a professional for the interview and shadowing, and reliable data. A familiar career can also score at the top if your research, interview insights and connections are deep. Choose the career you are most curious about and can investigate thoroughly, because enthusiasm shows in a five-minute presentation.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Match the lesser-known career to its work","pairs":[["Perfusionist","Runs the heart–lung machine during surgery"],["Orthotist and prosthetist","Designs and fits braces and artificial limbs"],["Nuclear medicine technologist","Prepares radioactive tracers and performs scans"],["Child life specialist","Helps children cope with hospital care through play"],["Polysomnographic technologist","Monitors patients during overnight sleep studies"],["Orthoptist","Evaluates and treats eye-movement disorders"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Choose one specific health career—not a specialty area or non-health job—with a learnable clinical skill.','Use pathways (therapeutic, diagnostic, informatics, support, biotechnology) and NCHSE, Explore Health Careers, BLS and O*NET to explore.','Make four or more specific connections between your strengths and the career, and check the full plan before committing.'],terms:[['Health career','An occupation whose main purpose is promoting, maintaining or restoring health.'],['Area of specialty','A field of practice, such as cardiology, that contains many careers.'],['Career pathway','A group of related careers sharing knowledge and skills.']],mcqs:[q('Which is acceptable as a Clinical Specialty career?','Medical examiner','Forensics','Firefighter','Special education teacher','HOSA gives medical examiner as an example of a career.'),q('“Cardiology” is best described as:','An area of specialty, not a specific career','A specific career','A non-health career','A support service','Choose a career such as cardiovascular technologist.'),q('Which pathway includes medical laboratory scientists?','Diagnostic services','Support services','Health informatics','Therapeutic services','They create a picture of health status.'),q('To earn full points for relating strengths, a competitor should identify:','Four or more strong connections','One general reason','Two weak connections','Only salary information','The rubric rewards several strong connections.'),q('Which resource lists an occupation’s tasks, skills and work styles?','O*NET OnLine','A social media poll','A video game','A dictionary','O*NET is a U.S. Department of Labor database.'),q('Which requirement must every chosen career meet?','It has at least one clinical skill the competitor can learn and demonstrate','It must be a physician career','It must require a doctoral degree','It must be in a hospital','The skill drives the video and checklist.'),q('Why check skill eligibility before committing to a career?','A duplicated skill earns zero on item 12 and forces rework','Judges prefer duplicated skills','Skills are chosen by judges','It does not matter','Plan the skill early.'),q('Which list did HOSA note was updated to match Advance CTE career clusters?','The NCHSE career list','The BLS wage list','The HOSA officer list','The AMA list','Review it carefully.')],frq:{prompt:'A classmate wants to compete with “pediatrics” and says the reason is “I like kids.” Explain why this choice and reasoning need revision, and guide them to an eligible career with strong personal connections.',rubric:[['Career vs specialty rule',['specialty','specific','career','pediatrics']],['Eligible example career',['pediatric','nurse','respiratory therapist','child life','therapist','assistant']],['Clinical skill and eligibility check',['skill','not duplicat','text','interview','shadow']],['Specific personal connections',['strength','connection','specific','four','o*net','experience']]],model:'“Pediatrics” is an area of specialty, not a specific career, so it does not meet HOSA’s rule; the classmate should choose a specific profession that works with children, such as pediatric respiratory therapist, child life specialist, pediatric nurse or pediatric occupational therapist. They must confirm the career has at least one clinical skill they can learn from a verifiable text and demonstrate safely, that the skill is not used in another HOSA event, and that they can find a practicing professional to interview and a site for 8 hours of job shadowing. “I like kids” is vague; using O*NET tasks and work styles, they should make four or more specific connections—for example, babysitting experience with a child who has asthma, patience in teaching, comfort with science classes and enjoyment of play-based communication—each tied to a duty of the chosen career.'},sources:[RULES,NCHSE,EHC,ONET]};

export const S3={title:'Selecting and sourcing the clinical skill',hook:'Your skill is judged three times: as a written checklist in the portfolio, as a recorded performance and as something you discuss live. Choose a skill that is not used in any other HOSA event, that truly represents the career and that comes step by step from a text you can cite.',objectives:['Apply HOSA’s skill-selection requirements, including the non-duplication rule.','Choose a skill that builds career awareness rather than a trivial task.','Identify a verifiable text resource and cite it on the checklist.','Plan a safe, realistic demonstration using a model or simulation.'],sections:[
['What the skill must be',[
'The skill must be **performed by professionals in the chosen career**, **clinical**, something the competitor **can learn and actually perform or simulate**, and **not verbalized only**—the competitor must be seen performing it in the video, and a model or simulation is acceptable when performed safely. It must **not duplicate** any skill used in another HOSA Health Professions or Emergency Preparedness event; the guidelines list those skills, and judges check the list for rating-sheet item 12 (5 points, all or nothing).',
'The skill must also **serve career awareness**. HOSA’s example: a nursing assistant may fax documents, but “faxing a document” is a poor choice because it does not help anyone understand the career. A good skill shows what makes the profession distinct—the reason someone would train for it.']],
['The non-duplication list',[
'The guidelines list skills from Biotechnology, Clinical Lab Science, CERT, Clinical Nursing, CPR/First Aid, Dental Science, EMT, Genetics, Home Health Aide, Medical Assisting, Nursing Assisting and Personal Care, Occupational Therapy, Patient Care Technician, Pharmacy Science, Phlebotomy, Physical Therapy, Respiratory Therapy, Sports Medicine, Surgical Technologist and Veterinary Science. Many common choices are on it: vital signs, handwashing, PPE, adult CPR, venipuncture, a 12-lead EKG, glucometer use, pulse oximetry, nebulizer treatment, crutch walking, passive range of motion, ankle taping, sterile gloving, surgical scrub and dental instrument identification. Competitors read the whole list—a similar skill with a new name is still a duplicate in spirit, and judges notice.',
sort('On the HOSA list (not allowed) or potentially allowed?',['On another event’s list','Potentially allowed after checking'],[['Measure and record vital signs',0],['Obtaining a 12-lead EKG',0],['Hand-held nebulizer treatment',0],['Ambulating with crutches',0],['Otoscopic inspection of the ear canal',1],['Measuring waist circumference for nutrition assessment',1],['Applying a wrist splint as an orthotist',1],['Sterile gloving',0]])]],
['Finding a verifiable text resource',[
'The checklist template requires a reference with **title, author, copyright and page numbers**, and the guidelines state that the skill **must come from a verifiable text resource and follow its steps**. A teacher, health professional or parent **cannot** serve as the skill resource, even if they taught you the skill. Good sources are current textbooks for the profession, published clinical procedure manuals and peer-reviewed or professional-association procedure guides. School and public libraries, career and technical education programs and community college bookstores can provide access. Keep the book or document at hand while writing the checklist so every step matches.',
chk('Which can serve as the skill resource?',['A published textbook for the profession with title, author, copyright and pages','The professional you shadowed','Your HOSA advisor’s handout from memory','A parent who works in the field'],'The skill must come from a verifiable text resource.')]],
['Representative, safe and demonstrable',[
'Ask three questions about each candidate skill. **Representative?** Would a judge watching it learn something essential about the career? **Safe?** Can it be simulated without risk—no real needles into people, no medications, no radiation, no invasive procedures on a volunteer—using a manikin, model or a volunteer only for noninvasive steps? **Demonstrable in a short clip?** Can enough of it be shown clearly within a five-minute presentation that also covers the career? Skills with visible, sequential steps—positioning, measuring, fitting, assessing—usually film well. Skills that happen mostly inside an instrument or a computer are hard to show.',
kase('Choosing between skills',{a:{text:'A student chose audiologist. Candidate skills: (1) scheduling a hearing test, (2) performing otoscopic inspection of the ear canal, (3) programming a cochlear implant. Which is best?',choices:[['Otoscopic inspection','b','Correct. It is clinical, representative, visible and can be safely simulated with proper technique.',true],['Scheduling a hearing test','b','Scheduling does not build career awareness.',false],['Programming a cochlear implant','b','It requires specialized equipment and a patient with an implant; it cannot be safely demonstrated.',false]]},b:{text:'What must the student confirm next?',choices:[['That otoscopy is not on HOSA’s list and that a verifiable text provides the steps','c','Correct. Check the list and find the textbook procedure.',true],['That a classmate can explain it','c','A person cannot serve as the resource.',false]]},c:{text:'The student finds the procedure in a published clinical skills text, confirms it is not listed and practices with a training otoscope.',end:true}})]],
['Building the skill into the whole event',[
'The skill links all three products. In the **portfolio**, it appears on the title page and as the skill checklist. In the **video**, the competitor performs the same steps from the checklist. In the **presentation**, the competitor explains the steps and why they matter—for example, why the ear is pulled up and back for an adult, or why the speculum size matters. The rubric’s video item rewards using the video “to reflect a deep understanding of the chosen career,” so practice explaining the rationale for key steps, not just performing them.',
match('Where does the skill appear?',[['Title page','Names the chosen skill'],['Skill checklist','At least 10 steps from a verifiable text'],['Skill video','Competitor visibly performs the checklist steps'],['Presentation','Discusses performance and rationale'],['Rating item 12','Confirms the skill is not duplicated'],['Reference on checklist','Title, author, copyright and page numbers']])]],
["Learning the skill to competence",["Knowing the steps is not the same as performing them smoothly. Professionals build skills through **deliberate practice**: read the procedure in the text and understand the purpose of each step; watch the step being done correctly, if possible during shadowing; practice slowly with the checklist beside you; then repeat until the sequence is automatic and you can explain each step while doing it. A qualified person—your shadowing host, a teacher or a health professional—may **coach** you and check your technique for safety, even though they cannot serve as the skill **resource**. Their feedback on hand position, communication and infection control can lift the quality of your video.","Practice under realistic conditions. Use the same equipment you will film with, set up the area as a clinician would and time yourself so you know how long each segment takes. Record practice runs and compare them with the checklist; most errors are omissions—skipping hand hygiene, forgetting to identify the patient or explain the procedure, or leaving out documentation. Practice the **communication** parts aloud, because professionals talk patients through skills. Finally, check safety limits: if any step would require an invasive action, simulate it on a manikin or model and say so clearly on camera.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Coaching or skill resource?","buckets":["Acceptable as coaching only","Acceptable as the skill resource"],"items":[["Your shadowing host watches you practice",0],["A published clinical procedures textbook",1],["A teacher corrects your hand position",0],["A professional-association procedure manual with author and date",1],["A parent who is a nurse gives tips",0]]}]]
],takeaways:['The skill must be clinical, from the chosen career, learnable, performed (not just verbalized) and not on another event’s list.','It must come from a verifiable text with title, author, copyright and pages—never a person.','Choose skills that are representative, safe to simulate and visible in a short clip.'],terms:[['Verifiable text resource','A published source anyone can check, such as a textbook.'],['Non-duplication','The rule that a skill may not already appear in another HOSA event.'],['Simulation','Safely performing a procedure on a model or without invasive steps.']],mcqs:[q('Which skill is NOT allowed because it appears in another event?','Measure and record vital signs','Otoscopic inspection','Waist circumference measurement','Fitting a wrist orthosis','Vital signs are in Nursing Assisting and other events.'),q('Who can serve as the skill resource?','A verifiable text resource','A teacher','A health professional','A parent','People cannot be the resource.'),q('Why is “faxing a document” a poor skill for a nursing assistant?','It does not promote understanding of the career','It is too difficult','It is not on the list','It takes too long','Skills must build career awareness.'),q('Which reference details are on the checklist template?','Title, author, copyright and page numbers','Only the website name','The judge’s name','The competitor’s grade','The template requires all four.'),q('A skill may be demonstrated:','On a model if performed safely, but not merely verbalized','Only by narrating','Only on a real patient','By a classmate','The competitor must be seen performing.'),q('How many points is “skill not duplicated” worth?','5, all or nothing','15','10 with partial credit','0','It is item 12.'),q('Which is the safest choice to film?','A noninvasive assessment on a volunteer or model','Giving a real injection to a friend','Inserting a catheter in a volunteer','Administering medication','Simulate invasive steps.'),q('In the presentation, the competitor should:','Explain the rationale for key steps of the skill','Only play the whole video silently','Read the checklist aloud without the video','Skip the skill','Understanding is scored.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would select, verify and prepare a clinical skill for Clinical Specialty for a career of your choice. Address eligibility, the source, safety and how the skill connects to the rest of the event.',rubric:[['Eligibility and non-duplication',['not duplicat','list','other event','clinical','career']],['Verifiable text source',['text','textbook','title','author','copyright','page']],['Safety and demonstration',['model','simulat','safe','visible','perform']],['Connection to portfolio, video and presentation',['checklist','video','presentation','rationale','title page']]],model:'For a career such as audiologist, I would list clinical skills the professional performs and choose one that is representative and visible, such as otoscopic inspection of the ear canal. I would check the guidelines’ full list of skills used in other HOSA events to confirm it is not duplicated, because item 12 is all or nothing. I would find the procedure in a published clinical skills textbook and record its title, author, copyright and page numbers, since a teacher or professional cannot be the source. To be safe, I would practice with a training otoscope on a model or a consenting volunteer, using gentle technique and correct speculum size, and never attempt anything invasive. The skill appears on the title page, becomes the 10-plus-step checklist, is performed visibly in the video following the same steps and is explained in the presentation, where I would discuss why key steps, like pulling the adult ear up and back, matter.'},sources:[RULES,EHC,ONET]};

export const S4={title:'Researching the career: summary, education and data',hook:'Two portfolio sections, worth 20 points together, ask for information anyone could look up—but judges award full credit only when it is complete, accurate and clearly organized. The difference between “average” and “excellent” is using authoritative sources and covering every criterion on the rating sheet.',objectives:['Write a career summary that covers all four rating-sheet criteria.','Explain education options, licensure, certification and registration.','Find and cite employment statistics and occupational outlook from authoritative sources.','Identify the professional associations for a career.'],sections:[
['The career summary (maximum two pages)',[
'The **career summary** earns 10 points for complete, clear and comprehensive information including **(1) a description of the career**, **(2) job duties**, **(3) employment characteristics** and **(4) at least one of**: career environment, technological needs, work schedule or personal characteristics. Covering only three criteria drops the score to 8; two to 6. Superficial or vague data also lowers the score. A strong summary opens with a one-sentence definition, describes a typical day and the range of duties (including the clinical skill you chose), explains where professionals work and with whom, notes the technology they use, describes schedules (shifts, weekends, on-call) and lists the personal characteristics that help someone succeed.',
sort('Which career-summary criterion does each sentence address?',['Description or duties','Employment characteristics or environment'],[['Respiratory therapists evaluate and treat patients with breathing disorders.',0],['They administer aerosol medications and manage ventilators.',0],['Most work in hospitals, often in 12-hour shifts including nights.',1],['They work closely with physicians and nurses in intensive care units.',1],['They teach patients to use inhalers and airway clearance devices.',0],['Ventilators and blood gas analyzers are core technology.',1]])]],
['Education, training and credentials',[
'The education section (also 10 points, maximum two pages) must cover **six** items: **educational requirements and options**, **credentialing requirements**, **professional association information**, **employment statistics**, **occupational outlook** and **additional career-related data**. Education options might include certificate programs, associate, bachelor’s, master’s or doctoral degrees, along with clinical rotations, residencies or apprenticeships. Note the difference between credentials:',
{t:'table',caption:'Types of health credentials',head:['Credential','Granted by','Meaning'],rows:[['Licensure','A state government agency or board','Legal permission to practice; required by law (for example, RN, physician, physical therapist)'],['Certification','A professional or nongovernmental organization','Shows the person met standards, often by exam; may be required by employers or states'],['Registration','A professional or state registry','Listing on an official register, sometimes after an exam (for example, registered dietitian)'],['Continuing education','Required by boards and certifying bodies','Ongoing learning to maintain a credential']]},
'The **CareerOneStop** license and certification finder, state board websites and certifying organizations identify which credentials apply where you plan to work. Accreditation agencies for education programs (such as CAAHEP for many allied health programs) indicate quality programs whose graduates can sit for credentialing exams.']],
['Employment statistics and outlook',[
'Use authoritative sources for numbers. The **BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook** gives each occupation’s median pay, typical entry-level education, number of jobs and projected employment change over ten years, with explanations of what drives demand. **O*NET OnLine** adds tasks, technology, work context and state wage data, and state labor departments provide local projections. Report the **source and year** for every statistic, because numbers change with each release. Interpret the data: explain *why* demand is growing (for example, an aging population, chronic disease or new technology) and what that means for job seekers.',
chk('A portfolio states “Jobs will grow a lot.” How could it be improved?',['Cite the BLS projected percent change, the projection period and the reason for growth','Remove all numbers','Use a social media post as the source','Copy a paragraph from a website without citation'],'Specific, sourced data earns full credit.')]],
['Professional associations',[
'Professional associations represent a career, set practice standards, publish journals, offer continuing education and often run certification or advocacy. Examples include the American Association for Respiratory Care, the American Occupational Therapy Association, the American Society of Radiologic Technologists, the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics and the American Speech-Language-Hearing Association. In the portfolio, name the association, describe what it does for members and students (student memberships, scholarships, conferences, code of ethics) and explain how it shapes the profession.',
match('Match the career to an association',[['Respiratory therapist','American Association for Respiratory Care'],['Occupational therapist','American Occupational Therapy Association'],['Radiologic technologist','American Society of Radiologic Technologists'],['Registered dietitian','Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics'],['Speech-language pathologist','American Speech-Language-Hearing Association'],['Physical therapist','American Physical Therapy Association']])]],
['Writing it well',[
'Both sections may use narrative or bulleted outline style but must use **section headers**. Keep within two pages each—judges stop reading excess pages and the whole portfolio must stay within 12 pages. Paraphrase and cite; do not paste paragraphs from websites. A short table can present education paths or statistics compactly, and the reference page lists every source in APA style. Accuracy matters: data that is “incorrect or questionable” scores only 4 of 10.',
kase('Fact-checking a draft',{a:{text:'A draft says, “Respiratory therapists need a medical degree and are licensed by the AMA.” What is wrong?',choices:[['Respiratory therapists need at least an associate degree, and licensure is granted by states, not the AMA','b','Correct. Licensure comes from state boards; credentials like RRT come from the NBRC.',true],['Nothing; the statement is accurate','b','A medical degree is not required, and the AMA does not license practitioners.',false]]},b:{text:'How should the competitor verify credentials?',choices:[['Check the state licensing board and the national credentialing organization’s website and cite them','c','Correct. Primary sources are most reliable.',true],['Ask a friend','c','Use authoritative, citable sources.',false]]},c:{text:'The corrected section describes associate and bachelor’s programs, the CRT and RRT credentials and state licensure, with sources cited.',end:true}})]],
["Evaluating sources and avoiding plagiarism",["Judges can tell when portfolio data comes from weak sources. Evaluate each source for **currency** (is it recent enough for employment or credential information?), **relevance** (does it describe your specific career and country or state?), **authority** (government agencies, accrediting bodies, credentialing organizations, professional associations and textbooks are strongest), **accuracy** (can the information be verified elsewhere?) and **purpose** (is it informing or selling—for example, a for-profit school’s advertisement?). Prefer **primary sources**: the BLS for employment data, the credentialing body for exam requirements, the state board for licensure.","**Plagiarism**—presenting someone else’s words or ideas as your own—includes copying sentences without quotation marks, lightly rewording a paragraph and failing to cite a source for a fact or statistic. Paraphrase by reading the passage, closing the source, writing the idea in your own words and then checking accuracy. In APA, cite in text with the author and year, such as (U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, 2026), and list the full reference on the reference page. Direct quotations should be short, in quotation marks and include page numbers when available.",{"t":"example","title":"Paraphrasing a statistic","prompt":"A government source states that employment for an occupation is projected to grow faster than average over the decade. How should a portfolio present this?","steps":["Identify the source and year of the projection.","Restate the idea in your own words and include the projected percentage and period.","Add the reason for growth that the source gives.","Add an in-text citation and a full reference entry."],"answer":"For example: “Employment of respiratory therapists is projected to grow faster than the average for all occupations over the next decade, largely because an aging population will have more respiratory conditions (U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, 2026).”"}]]
],takeaways:['Career summary (10 points): description, duties, employment characteristics plus environment, technology, schedule or personal traits.','Education section (10 points): education, credentials, association, statistics, outlook and additional data.','Use BLS, O*NET, state boards and associations; cite source and year for every number.'],terms:[['Licensure','Government permission to practice a profession.'],['Certification','Credential from a professional organization showing standards were met.'],['Occupational outlook','Projected change in employment over a period.']],mcqs:[q('How many criteria does the career summary rating require for full points?','Four','Two','Six','Ten','Description, duties, employment characteristics and one more trait.'),q('How many criteria does the education and data section list?','Six','Three','Four','Eight','Education, credentials, association, statistics, outlook and additional data.'),q('Licensure is granted by:','A state government agency or board','A professional association','A school club','An employer','It is legal permission to practice.'),q('Which source provides median pay and ten-year employment projections?','BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook','A personal blog','A hospital billboard','A fitness app','It is published by the U.S. Department of Labor.'),q('What is the maximum length of the career summary?','Two pages','One page','Four pages','Unlimited','Excess pages count against the limit.'),q('Data that is incorrect or questionable earns:','4 of 10 points','10 of 10','8 of 10','Bonus points','Accuracy matters.'),q('Which statement about certification is true?','It comes from a nongovernmental organization and shows standards were met','It is always a legal requirement to practice','It is the same as a degree','It never requires an exam','Certification and licensure differ.'),q('Why include the year of a statistic?','Data changes with each release','Judges like dates','It replaces the citation','It is not needed','Cite source and year.')],frq:{prompt:'Outline the career summary and education/data sections for a health career of your choice, showing how you would meet every rating-sheet criterion and which sources you would use.',rubric:[['Career summary criteria',['description','duties','employment','environment','schedule','technology']],['Education and credentials',['degree','program','licens','certif','registr']],['Statistics and outlook with sources',['bls','outlook','median','projected','o*net','source']],['Association and presentation',['association','header','two pages','cite','apa']]],model:'For an occupational therapy assistant, my career summary would define the career (helping patients regain daily living skills under an occupational therapist), describe duties such as leading therapeutic activities and teaching adaptive techniques, explain employment characteristics like work in hospitals, schools, skilled nursing and home health, and add work schedule, technology such as adaptive equipment, and personal traits like patience. The education section would describe the associate degree from an ACOTE-accredited program with fieldwork, the NBCOT certification exam and state licensure, and the American Occupational Therapy Association with its student membership and code of ethics. I would add median pay, number of jobs and the projected ten-year growth rate from the BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook, explain growth from an aging population, and include state wage data from O*NET. Each section would use headers, stay within two pages, paraphrase sources and cite them in APA style with the year of the data.'},sources:[RULES,OOH,ONET,COS]};

export const S5={title:'Interviewing a health professional',hook:'The interview summary is worth 10 points, and the rating sheet’s top band demands information that “can only be learned through conversation” with a professional. A summary built from questions a search engine could answer scores as low as 2. The skill is asking questions that open up a professional’s experience.',objectives:['Identify an eligible professional and arrange an interview professionally.','Write questions that draw out information not available online.','Conduct the interview with respect for time and confidentiality.','Write a narrative summary that meets every rating-sheet criterion.'],sections:[
['Who you may interview',[
'The interview must be with a **practicing health professional in your chosen field**. It **may not** be your instructor or HOSA advisor, and a summary that obviously used the local advisor scores zero. A **virtual interview** may be acceptable in some cases. The summary must include the professional’s **name, job title, work location and experience**. Contact sources include your shadowing site, local hospitals’ volunteer or education offices, professional association chapters, community colleges’ program directors and family or community connections—but the person must actually practice in the career.',
chk('Which person is an eligible interviewee for a competitor studying radiologic technology?',['A practicing radiologic technologist at a local imaging center','The competitor’s HOSA advisor, a former technologist','A radiology textbook author who no longer practices','A classmate who plans to become a technologist'],'The interviewee must be a practicing professional and not your instructor or advisor.')]],
['Arranging the interview professionally',[
'First impressions matter. Request the interview by email or phone with a short, polite message: introduce yourself and HOSA, explain the purpose (a career-awareness portfolio), request 20–30 minutes in person or virtually, offer flexible times and thank them. Confirm the day before. Dress professionally, arrive or log in early, bring your questions and a notebook, and **ask permission** before recording. After the interview, send a **thank-you note** within a day or two. These habits are the same ones the Work-based Learning Form evaluates for shadowing.',
order('Professional interview process',['Identify an eligible practicing professional','Send a polite request explaining HOSA and the purpose','Confirm date, time and format; prepare questions','Arrive early, dressed professionally','Ask permission before recording or taking photos','Ask questions and listen actively, taking notes','Thank them and send a thank-you note','Write the narrative summary while details are fresh'])]],
['Questions that go beyond the internet',[
'Facts like typical salary, degree requirements and job outlook are easy to find online; they belong in your research section. The interview should explore **experience, judgment and reality**: what surprised them about the job, a challenging situation and how they handled it, how their day really unfolds, how they work with other team members, how technology has changed their work, what they wish they had known as a student, the most rewarding moment of their career, what qualities make someone successful, and how they perform the skill you selected—including common mistakes. Follow-up questions (“Can you give an example?” “How did that feel?”) show **thoughtful questioning**, a criterion on the rating sheet.',
sort('Researchable fact or interview-worthy question?',['Researchable online (use in research section)','Interview-worthy (only a professional can answer)'],[['What is the median salary?',0],['What degree is required?',0],['What surprised you most in your first year?',1],['Describe a difficult patient interaction and what you learned.',1],['What is the ten-year job outlook?',0],['What mistakes do new graduates make with this skill?',1],['How does your team communicate during emergencies?',1],['Which organization certifies this career?',0]])]],
['Confidentiality and ethics',[
'Professionals must protect patient privacy under **HIPAA**, so never ask for patient names or identifying details, and do not include any in your summary. If a story is shared, describe it generally (“a teenage patient with asthma”). Respect the professional’s time and boundaries; if they decline a question, move on. Represent what they said accurately—do not exaggerate or invent quotes. If you quote them directly, make sure the quote is exact.',
kase('An identifying detail',{a:{text:'During the interview, a nurse practitioner mentions a memorable patient by first name and the town where the patient lives. How should this appear in your summary?',choices:[['Describe the situation without the name or location, such as “an older adult with heart failure”','b','Correct. Remove identifying details.',true],['Include the name to make the story vivid','b','Including identifying details violates privacy expectations.',false]]},b:{text:'The nurse practitioner asks you not to record. What do you do?',choices:[['Agree and take careful written notes','c','Correct. Always respect consent.',true],['Record secretly on your phone','c','Recording without permission is unethical and may be illegal.',false]]},c:{text:'Your notes capture the main lessons, and your summary protects privacy while showing what you learned.',end:true}})]],
['Writing the interview summary (maximum two pages)',[
'The summary must be a **narrative**, not a transcript. The rating sheet’s top band requires **(1)** a career-related interview with a professional in the specific field, including name, workplace and profession; **(2)** **thoughtful questioning and comprehension** of the answers; and **(3)** **specific information that can only be learned through conversation**. A plain question-and-answer list, or a summary of mostly online information, scores only 4 points. Organize the narrative by theme (daily reality, challenges, teamwork, the selected skill, advice) and connect what you learned to your career choice and to your shadowing experience. Show comprehension by explaining *why* an answer matters.',
flip('Transcript vs. narrative',[['Q: What is hard about your job? A: Long shifts.','“Ms. Rivera explained that twelve-hour night shifts were the hardest adjustment; she protects her sleep with a fixed routine, which made me realize self-care is a professional skill.”'],['Q: Do you like your job? A: Yes.','“She described the moment a ventilated patient she had cared for walked back to visit the ICU, which she called the reason she stays.”'],['Q: What skills matter? A: Communication.','“She stressed explaining each step before touching a patient, and demonstrated how she adjusts her language for children.”']]),
chk('Which summary feature moves the score from 4 to 10 points?',['Narrative with insights only a professional could provide and evidence of thoughtful follow-up questions','A list of questions and one-word answers','Salary and degree information copied from a website','A photo of the professional'],'The rubric rewards depth, comprehension and conversation-only information.')]],
["Listening, virtual interviews and a model paragraph",["**Active listening** turns a good question into a great answer. Make eye contact, nod, allow silence after the professional finishes and ask follow-ups that build on what you heard: “You mentioned the first month was overwhelming—what helped you adjust?” Take brief notes of key phrases and examples rather than trying to write every word, then expand them immediately after the interview. If you do not understand a term, ask; comprehension is scored.","For a **virtual interview**, test the platform and your camera and microphone in advance, choose a quiet, well-lit space with a neutral background, dress professionally, look at the camera when speaking and have a backup plan (a phone number) if the connection fails. Confirm the time zone. The same expectations for permission, privacy and a thank-you note apply.",{"t":"example","title":"From notes to narrative","prompt":"Notes: “RT, 9 yrs, Children’s Hospital PICU. Hardest: parents watching during emergencies. Explains each step calmly. Uses stuffed animals to show masks. Advice: learn to stay calm.” Write a narrative paragraph.","steps":["Introduce the professional with name, title, workplace and experience.","Present the insight in narrative form with an example.","Show comprehension by explaining why it matters.","Connect it to your own goals or skill."],"answer":"“Ms. Lee, a registered respiratory therapist with nine years in a children’s hospital intensive care unit, explained that the hardest moments are emergencies with parents present. She narrates each step in a calm voice so families understand what is happening, and she uses a stuffed animal to show children how a mask fits before placing it on them. Her approach showed me that communication is a clinical skill, not an extra, and I practiced explaining each step of my own skill aloud for the same reason.”"}]]
],takeaways:['Interview a practicing professional in your field—never your instructor or HOSA advisor; include name, title, workplace and experience.','Ask questions only conversation can answer, with follow-ups; protect privacy and ask before recording.','Write a themed narrative showing comprehension, not a Q&A transcript.'],terms:[['Narrative summary','A written account organized by ideas rather than a list of questions.'],['Follow-up question','A question that builds on an answer to gain depth.'],['Informed consent (recording)','Permission obtained before recording a conversation.']],mcqs:[q('Who may NOT be interviewed?','The competitor’s HOSA advisor','A practicing professional in the field','A professional met during shadowing','A professional interviewed virtually','Instructors and advisors are excluded.'),q('Which details must the summary include?','Name, job title, work location and experience of the professional','The professional’s home address','Patient names','The professional’s salary','These show the interview is genuine.'),q('A summary written as a list of questions and short answers scores about:','4 points','10 points','15 points','0 points always','A Q&A format falls in the average band.'),q('Which question is best for the interview?','What surprised you most in your first year?','What is the median salary?','What degree is required?','How many jobs exist nationally?','The others can be researched online.'),q('Before recording an interview, you should:','Ask permission','Record secretly','Post it online','Skip notes','Respect consent.'),q('How should a patient story appear?','Without names or identifying details','With the patient’s name','With the hospital room number','With a photo','Protect privacy.'),q('What is the maximum length of the interview summary?','Two pages','One page','Five pages','Half a page','Stay within the section limit.'),q('Why send a thank-you note?','It shows professionalism and respect for the professional’s time','It is required for points','It replaces the summary','It is not appropriate','Professional habits matter.')],frq:{prompt:'Plan an interview for your chosen health career: identify an eligible professional, write five questions that cannot be answered by online research, and explain how you would turn the interview into a summary that earns full points.',rubric:[['Eligible professional and arrangement',['practicing','not','advisor','instructor','email','request']],['Five conversation-only questions',['surprise','challenge','advice','example','day','skill']],['Ethics and conduct',['permission','record','privacy','hipaa','thank']],['Summary meeting the rubric',['narrative','name','title','location','experience','comprehension','theme']]],model:'For a career as a respiratory therapist, I would ask the education coordinator at a local hospital to connect me with a practicing respiratory therapist, avoiding my instructor or HOSA advisor, and send a polite email introducing HOSA and requesting 30 minutes. My questions would be: What surprised you most in your first year? Can you describe a time you had to make a quick decision with a ventilated patient? How do you teach a frightened child to use an inhaler? What mistakes do new therapists make with the skill I chose? What do you wish you had known as a student? I would ask permission before recording, never request patient identifiers and send a thank-you note afterward. My summary would be a narrative organized by themes, naming the therapist, title, workplace and years of experience, explaining what each answer taught me and why it matters, and including details only a conversation could reveal—showing thoughtful follow-up and comprehension.'},sources:[RULES,HIPAA,EHC]};
